 ],
 "literal": "Soft-natured dark peacock will shiver, thinking so, having pitied / and given a shawl, O Began of undying good fame, / rutting elephant, and spirited horses! / Even being hungry we do not come, and we do not have dependents indeed. / Like the black kalavu fruit, the black-stemmed small lute, / making those who play desiring melody vibrate, having tuned, / 'Please do righteousness, O you who desire grace!' saying, / this is the gift we requested; that is, in the darkness, / mounting the tall chariot with grouped bells, / please remove the deep grief of her who dwells painfully.",
 "translation": "O Began of undying fame, who possesses rutting elephants and spirited horses, you who once pitied a soft-natured dark peacock thinking it was shivering and wrapped it in your shawl! We do not come to you out of hunger, nor do we have a burden of dependents. Tuning our small lute with its black stem resembling the dark kalavu fruit, making its sweet melody vibrate, we sing: 'O you who love to show grace, do what is righteous!' This is the only gift we beg of you: mount your tall chariot with its ringing bells, and go in the dark of night to remove the deep grief of your wife who lives in painful solitude.",
 "modern": "மென்மையான இயல்புடைய கரிய மயில் குளிரால் நடுங்கும் என்று இரக்கப்பட்டு அதற்குப் போர்வையை அளித்த, அழியாத நல்ல புகழையும், மதயானைகளையும், கலிமா குதிரைகளையும் உடைய பேகனே! நாங்கள் பசியோடு வரவில்லை; எங்களுக்குச் சுற்றத்தாராகிய பாரமும் இல்லை. களாப்பழம் போன்ற கரிய தண்டினைக் கொண்ட சிறிய யாழில் இனிய இசையை விரும்பி வாசிப்பவர் நடுங்கும்படி பண்ணி, 'அருளை விரும்பும் வள்ளலே! அறத்தைச் செய்வாயாக!' என்று பாடினோம். யாம் உன்னிடம் இரந்து கேட்கும் பரிசு இதுவே: இந்த இருள் நிறைந்த இரவிலே, கூட்டமாகிய மணிகளைக் கட்டிய நெடிய தேரில் ஏறிச் சென்று, உன்னைப் பிரிந்து துன்பத்துடன் வாழும் உன் மனைவியின் அரிய துயரத்தைப் போக்குவாயாக!",
 "explanation": "This poem is addressed to the chieftain Vēḷ Pēkaṉ by the poet Kapilar. Pēkaṉ was famous for his extraordinary generosity, famously giving his expensive shawl to a peacock thinking it was shivering from the cold. However, he had abandoned his wife, Kaṇṇaki, and was living with another woman. Kapilar, along with other poets, visited Pēkaṉ not to beg for wealth or food, but to urge him to return to his grieving wife. The poet tunes his small lute (cīṟiyāḻ) and sings that the only gift (paricil) they desire from him is for him to mount his chariot and go back to his wife to relieve her deep sorrow (arumpaṭar). This poem belongs to the Peruintiṇai category as it deals with mismatched or improper love/separation, specifically the poet's intervention in a domestic crisis.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-27"
}